# Welcome!

#### This professional learning module prepares you to teach a unit where students expand their understanding of web apps by connecting them to external data sources through APIs. They learn that modern applications rarely operate in isolation; instead, they depend on information and services provided by larger systems.

#### An **API (Application Programming Interface)** is a set of rules that allow software applications to communicate and use data from another server. 


___

<div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; width: 95%; margin: 20pt auto; padding: 0 5px;">
  <img src="https://images.code.org/36211eedb3698751fa5523b4dde93351-image-1760467094025.png" style="border-radius: 5pt; width: 32%;">
  <img src="https://images.code.org/7f813b0aec7d472e470f94eb91471aa1-image-1760466945803.png" style="border-radius: 5pt; width: 32%;">
  <img src="https://images.code.org/ddb463a06ccc88f5d7390a1f841a0f31-image-1760467013014.png" style="border-radius: 5pt; width: 32%;">
</div>

___


#### **By the end of this professional learning module, you will be able to:**

* #### Use JavaScript to get data from an external API and show it in a web app.

* #### Write asynchronous code with `async/await` to load data without freezing the page.

* #### Compare using local data versus API data and explain when each makes sense.

* #### Get a solid introduction of APIs to prepare you to teach Unit 5.





